{:edb/db-before-t 395 :edb/db-after-t 396 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533708 50 #inst "2026-10-04T17:58:48.383Z" 13194139533708 true] [17592186152161 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247766 13194139533708 true] [17592186152161 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247773 13194139533708 true] [17592186152161 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247780 13194139533708 true] [17592186152161 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247787 13194139533708 true] [17592186152162 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247794 13194139533708 true] [17592186152162 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247801 13194139533708 true] [17592186152162 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247808 13194139533708 true] [17592186152162 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247815 13194139533708 true] [17592186152163 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247822 13194139533708 true] [17592186152163 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247829 13194139533708 true] [17592186152163 1020 #edb/ref 17592186247836 13194139533708 true] [17592186152338 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533708 true] [17592186152338 1145 "The plot shows that the data points are scattered along a straight line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938989/assets/c8597282ec934e535a01fa6f22be95b958d0393a5d0c5cd6358a9a7e620ba6a5.png)\n\nWe see that the line $q$ best fits the given data set." 13194139533708 true] [17592186152341 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533708 true] [17592186152341 1145 "Notice that the trend line passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(4,1)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(8,9).$\n\nTherefore, we compute the slope $m$ of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-1}{8-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533708 true] [17592186152342 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533708 true] [17592186152342 1145 "First, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938989/assets/0575edf80f4a6f26c8f1002404ac04331d4c5d1f81b644021bc6284a6212338a.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})≈(2,2)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})≈(6,8).$\n\nTherefore, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ≈\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-2}{6-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533708 true] [17592186152343 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533708 true] [17592186152343 1145 "First, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938989/assets/fd3ce3de75fa9296c721e3f3a28bef241eae6350819c4cc7a54707921c0c1fe4.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})≈(2,7)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})≈(8,4).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ≈\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-7}{8-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{6} \\\\  & =-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to estimate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,7):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+b \\\\ 7 & =-0.5(2)+b \\\\ 7 & =-1+b \\\\ 8 & =b \\\\ b & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+8.\n$$" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247766 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247767 13194139533708 true] [17592186247766 1089 "q-133639" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247766 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533708 true] [17592186247766 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938989/assets/3e0b0fc97e260decaa74a9a751b6422714d1ca7365aff3cc05e57f8904b9067a.png)\n\nA company produces cars. The scatter plot above shows the correspondence between the number of cars produced each week and the company's profit, in thousands of dollars. Which of the following is the trend line of the scatter plot?" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247766 1113 #uuid "2e76b127-8261-5bdd-91f7-28d466689876" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247766 1138 false 13194139533708 true] [17592186247766 1145 "The plot shows that the data points are scattered along a curve, not a straight line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938989/assets/e960deb7d006d665abb97a83791204190af503f98dc3a23eae407e6c03e155df.png)\n\nTherefore, there is no linear trend in the data." 13194139533708 true] [17592186247767 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247768 13194139533708 true] [17592186247767 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247769 13194139533708 true] [17592186247767 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247770 13194139533708 true] [17592186247767 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247771 13194139533708 true] [17592186247767 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247772 13194139533708 true] [17592186247767 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533708 true] [17592186247767 1041 "selection" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247767 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247769 13194139533708 true] [17592186247767 1110 #uuid "0330203f-f0fc-50d7-938c-74c52b241237" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247768 1034 "q" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247768 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533708 true] [17592186247768 1111 #uuid "08f3b38d-154c-5b12-9ffe-6be66ce0a9bd" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247769 1034 "There is no linear trend in the data" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247769 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533708 true] [17592186247769 1111 #uuid "c2877d37-c07a-551b-a319-ca8b4b36ae30" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247770 1034 "s" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247770 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533708 true] [17592186247770 1111 #uuid "c0865105-f19f-5352-822b-6049e0226937" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247771 1034 "r" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247771 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533708 true]