h the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,9)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(7,3).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the slope $m$ of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-9}{7-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{7} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-\\frac{6}{7}$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{6}{7}x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(0,9):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{6}{7}x+b \\\\ 9 & =-\\frac{6}{7}(0)+b \\\\ 9 & =0+b \\\\ b & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{6}{7}x+9.\n$$" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247809 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247810 13194139533708 true] [17592186247809 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247811 13194139533708 true] [17592186247809 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247812 13194139533708 true] [17592186247809 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247813 13194139533708 true] [17592186247809 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247814 13194139533708 true] [17592186247809 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533708 true] [17592186247809 1041 "selection" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247809 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247811 13194139533708 true] [17592186247809 1110 #uuid "baa20bd2-7fc3-54ff-a621-afe065059849" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247810 1034 "y=-\\frac{5}{7}x+8" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247810 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533708 true] [17592186247810 1111 #uuid "dca10fd8-90c8-5c9f-991c-b72c852548ea" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247811 1034 "y=-\\frac{6}{7}x+9" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247811 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533708 true] [17592186247811 1111 #uuid "271c1441-92dd-5749-a225-2cc308cdca1a" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247812 1034 "y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+9" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247812 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533708 true] [17592186247812 1111 #uuid "ba58e62d-472c-5500-97ca-e724a8e2c73e" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247813 1034 "y=-\\frac{6}{7}x+8" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247813 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533708 true] [17592186247813 1111 #uuid "dd475ef0-8f46-580c-9077-d17e7f8758b6" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247814 1034 "y=-\\frac{5}{7}x+9" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247814 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533708 true] [17592186247814 1111 #uuid "70379917-c5bd-5434-8cbc-f3d8ee725056" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247815 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247816 13194139533708 true] [17592186247815 1089 "q-134023" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247815 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533708 true] [17592186247815 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938989/assets/1c9f30692222eaaed4cda6b608dcc1ccb01eefd6b5fa7730b5b8bb29c9f528b1.png)\n\nDetermine the equation of the trend line shown above." 13194139533708 true] [17592186247815 1113 #uuid "e2360671-96ab-5521-825d-76752c839c61" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247815 1138 false 13194139533708 true] [17592186247815 1145 "Notice that the trend line passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,2)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(7,8).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the slope $m$ of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-2}{7-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=\\frac{6}{5}$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{6}{5}x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{6}{5}x+b \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{6}{5}(2)+b \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{12}{5}+b \\\\ 10 & =12+5b \\\\ -2 & =5b \\\\ b & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{6}{5}x-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247816 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247817 13194139533708 true] [17592186247816 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247818 13194139533708 true] [17592186247816 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247819 13194139533708 true] [17592186247816 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247820 13194139533708 true] [17592186247816 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247821 13194139533708 true] [17592186247816 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533708 true] [17592186247816 1041 "selection" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247816 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247820 13194139533708 true] [17592186247816 1110 #uuid "2c1abdaa-816d-5b68-a847-d70b8dee2562" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247817 1034 "y=\\frac{6}{5}x-\\frac{3}{5}" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247817 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533708 true] [17592186247817 1111 #uuid "7e78102d-ff88-586b-ba3a-50c68ff0b439" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247818 1034 "y=\\frac{7}{5}x-\\frac{2}{5}" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247818 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533708 true] [17592186247818 1111 #uuid "99ff009d-84f3-52e2-9e75-d2c333669fc3" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247819 1034 "y=\\frac{8}{5}x-\\frac{3}{5}" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247819 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533708 true] [17592186247819 1111 #uuid "5700f2a2-25ac-520b-b009-f748c7211115" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247820 1034 "y=\\frac{6}{5}x-\\frac{2}{5}" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247820 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533708 true] [17592186247820 1111 #uuid "138dc2ad-1379-5fa1-837a-4735ef7fc21f" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247821 1034 "y=\\frac{7}{5}x-\\frac{3}{5}" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247821 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533708 true] [17592186247821 1111 #uuid "5d19758b-bd47-52c6-a3a4-0ac4db2868c5" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247822 1017 #edb/ref 17592186247823 13194139533708 true] [17592186247822 1089 "q-134031" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247822 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533708 true] [17592186247822 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938989/assets/111833881cf8e939d896f23a5ab7ca436f9265e7b8fd66c099eb118eab8fd6b0.png)\n\nEstimate the equation of the trend line for the scatter plot above. Round both the slope and the $y$-intercept to the nearest $0.5.$" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247822 1113 #uuid "4ea3c63e-d2ec-56ec-acdc-942ec91b6a05" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247822 1138 false 13194139533708 true] [17592186247822 1145 "First, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938989/assets/fdad2c939372e14617ba4e393037d7f5852cd8114467aa914ccb8963c0eacfeb.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})≈(2,5)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})≈(6,1).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ≈\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-5}{6-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{4} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-1$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to estimate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-x+b \\\\ 5 & =-2+b \\\\ 7 & =b \\\\ b & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-x+7.\n$$" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247823 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247824 13194139533708 true] [17592186247823 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247825 13194139533708 true] [17592186247823 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247826 13194139533708 true] [17592186247823 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247827 13194139533708 true] [17592186247823 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247828 13194139533708 true] [17592186247823 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533708 true] [175921862478