per/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938989/assets/663e4d77d51816641a9fa861f37893d9f986d5e2b00e605ed5eb4fc19d40eab4.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})≈(5,5)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})≈(7,9).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ≈\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-5}{7-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=2$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=2x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to estimate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(5,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x+b \\\\ 5 & =2(5)+b \\\\ 5 & =10+b \\\\ -5 & =b \\\\ b & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=2x-5.\n$$" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247830 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247831 13194139533708 true] [17592186247830 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247832 13194139533708 true] [17592186247830 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247833 13194139533708 true] [17592186247830 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247834 13194139533708 true] [17592186247830 1000 #edb/ref 17592186247835 13194139533708 true] [17592186247830 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533708 true] [17592186247830 1041 "selection" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247830 1104 #edb/ref 17592186247834 13194139533708 true] [17592186247830 1110 #uuid "ede40fa3-2a42-5d3c-a69c-3f29eebcee88" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247831 1034 "y=2x-3" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247831 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533708 true] [17592186247831 1111 #uuid "0c4da729-75ff-5299-af69-9e8ac36242a1" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247832 1034 "y=2.5x-2" 13194139533708 true] [17592186247832 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533708 true] [17592186247832 1111 #uuid "df756c2b-c704-5ad5-9a24-ec07cdf51a7f" 13194139533708 tr